The Press (Christchurch, New Zealand) 1/11/94
=============================================

"University acts to curtail abusive electronic mail"
by Colin Espiner

Canterbury University has suspended two students from using its
computer system amid concerns that electronic discussion groups are
being used to inflict personal abuse.

The head of the university's computer services department, Robin
Harrington, said yesterday that he had become concerned at the
"immature" level of discussion in a campus-only news group and at the
invective being levelled at individual users.

After a staff member was recently warned by a user on the computer
system "We know where you live and we're angry", Mr Harrington
suspended a student for 24 hours. Several months earier a student was
suspended for five days for using obscene language in a news group.
The university has received complaints on an international user group
by a Canterbury student that was considered racist.

Mr Harrington said the local user group had been taken over by a small
number of students who directed abuse at anyone who "raised their head
above the horizon". He himself had also been the target of abuse.

The univesity took action against a spread of pornography on its
computer files last year, and some computer students say it has gone
too far and is now limiting academic freedom.

A PhD maths student, Chris Stephens, said yesterday that as well as
disiplining students, the university had banned user groups dealing
with safe sex, erotic art, and tasteless pictures.

Mr Stephens said many students used obscene language every day and
computer mail was no exception. "They banned everything with the word
sex in it. The university, acting on bad media coverage last year,
went to an extreme they shouldn't have done," he said.

Academic freedom and freedom of speech were in danger from the
university's actions. Even pornography had a place on the computer
system as it could be studied for legitimate academic purposes, Mr
Stephens said.

Mr harrington said the university was still grappling with a set of
guidelines to set the limits of discussion and what rights students
had to appeal decisions.

